# Line endings are LF in the working tree on every platform, not merely in the index.
#
# Git for Windows defaults to `core.autocrlf=true`, so without this a Windows checkout writes CRLF
# — and oxfmt formats to LF, so `vp check` then reports every text file in the repository as
# misformatted. Measured here: 93 of 93 files on the first CI run, including `Cargo.toml` and
# `.vscode/extensions.json`.
#
# The failure hides on the machine that made the commit, because `vp check --fix` rewrites those
# files to LF in place and git compares them normalised: `git status` stays clean while the bytes on
# disk no longer match what a fresh checkout would produce. It therefore surfaces first on a CI
# runner or a new clone. The tell, if this line is ever removed: git printing "LF will be replaced
# by CRLF the next time Git touches it" against files nobody deliberately changed.
* text=auto eol=lf

# The lockfile is generated and enormous; a diff of it helps nobody.
pnpm-lock.yaml -diff

# Binary assets must be exempt from eol normalization. `text=auto` above misdetects some of these as
# text, and git then strips `\r` bytes on checkout, corrupting them in the working tree while the
# stored blobs stay intact. In a sibling repo that broke 10 of 16 binary files and failed the
# build outright with "Input file contains unsupported image format".
#
# It misdetects only *some* binaries, so a repo with no exemptions and no visibly broken files has
# live risk rather than live damage — none of this repository's 17 tracked binaries is currently
# affected, which is not evidence the rules are unnecessary. The list is copied whole rather than
# extended one extension at a time, because the first uncovered type to be committed is the one that
# arrives corrupted.
*.png  binary
*.jpg  binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif  binary
*.ico  binary
*.icns binary
*.avif binary
*.webp binary
*.pdf  binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
*.ttf  binary
*.otf  binary
*.eot  binary
*.mp4  binary
*.webm binary
*.zip  binary
